Q: General advice / Other A: <p><code>std::numeric_limits&lt;int&gt;::infinity() == 0</code>. That check will never be true. <code>int</code> type doesn't have an infinity value; only floating-point types do.</p>
<p>The whole program is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>int main () {
  for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 100; ++i) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; i*i;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>What is this supposed to demonstrate? What does this have to do with big-O notation, or with computing square roots?</p>

Q: How to isolate isPending state from useActionState to prevent full form re-renders in React 19? A: <p>The performance bottleneck occurs because <code>useActionState</code> (and <code>useFormStatus</code>) are tied to the component's render cycle. If your hook is at the top level of a large form, any state change (like transition to <code>isPending: true</code>) forces the whole tree to re-evaluate.</p>
<p>The most efficient way to solve this in React 19 is using the <strong>&quot;Action-Isolation&quot; pattern</strong>. Instead of putting the hook in the main form component, you wrap the &quot;Submit&quot; logic in a specialized sub-component.</p>
<p>1. The &quot;Heavy&quot; Form (Uncontrolled):</p>
<p>Keep your inputs uncontrolled so they don't trigger re-renders on every keystroke.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function BigForm() {
  return (
    &lt;form action={formAction}&gt;
      {/* 100+ Inputs here */}
      &lt;input name=&quot;field_1&quot; defaultValue=&quot;Standard&quot; /&gt;

      {/* Move action state logic here */}
      &lt;SubmitHandler /&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>2. The Isolated Handler:</p>
<p>Use <code>useFormStatus</code> inside a child component. This hook specifically listens to the parent <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> without affecting the rest of the siblings.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function SubmitHandler() {
  const { pending, data } = useFormStatus();

  return (
    &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; disabled={pending}&gt;
      {pending ? 'Saving...' : 'Submit'}
    &lt;/button&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why this works:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Keystrokes: Since the inputs are uncontrolled, typing doesn't trigger any React state change.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Submission: When the action starts, only <code>SubmitHandler</code> re-renders to show the loading state. The hundreds of inputs in <code>BigForm</code> are completely ignored by the React reconciler during the pending state.</p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: Update &lt;selectedcontent&gt; from DOM change A: <p>Try to use a MutationObserver in your content script to detect DOM changes. When a change occurs, dispatch a custom event that your native wrapper can hook into to re-query the AXSelectedText or AXContents attribute.</p>
<pre><code>const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) =&gt; {
    // Notify the system that the &quot;Selection&quot; or &quot;Content&quot; is potentially stale
    window.postMessage({ type: 'DOM_UPDATED' }, '*');
});

observer.observe(document.body, { 
    childList: true, 
    subtree: true, 
    characterData: true 
});
</code></pre>
